WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:05.730
We're almost 20 installments deep into Nintendo's
Legend of Zelda series, not counting those

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.390
terrible CD-i games…

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:08.390
"It's awful.

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:11.809
Mayor Kravindish has been chained in the tower.

00:00:11.809 --> 00:00:13.140
Oh, my cakes!"

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.130
...and we're still going strong.

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:19.210
Breath of the Wild brought in advance buzz
by the heart-container-ful, and when fans

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:23.980
finally played for themselves, they noted
its balance between originality and honoring

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:25.499
the overall Zelda theme.

00:00:25.499 --> 00:00:30.149
But what some players likely haven't yet noticed
are the exciting connections to Zelda's legacy

00:00:30.149 --> 00:00:32.040
sprinkled throughout the game.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:33.989
Does Dodongo still dislike smoke?

00:00:33.989 --> 00:00:37.180
Are empty jars still a weirdly rare commodity
in Hyrule?

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.469
Will we ever figure out what the deal is with
this guy?

00:00:39.469 --> 00:00:44.280
Here's a look at a few small details in Breath
of the Wild only true fans noticed.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.559
Location, location, location

00:00:46.559 --> 00:00:50.750
Since its initial release over 30 years ago,
The Legend of Zelda has been all about the

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.199
lush landscapes.

00:00:52.199 --> 00:00:56.859
And amongst those grassy knolls and spooky
caves are a few structures that stand out.

00:00:56.859 --> 00:01:01.570
Easily one of the most recognizable is Spectacle
Rock, which first appeared in the original

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:05.800
1986 release, and has cropped up in later
installments as well.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:10.050
The double mountain appears again in Breath
of the Wild, where it can be seen in wide-angled

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:11.300
shots from above.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.870
But this time, the famous formation appears
on a much larger scale.

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:20.110
You might also recognize Ranch Ruins as Lon
Lon Ranch from Ocarina of Time, and fans will

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:23.110
probably get chills when they run into the
Temple of Time again.

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:27.290
Breath of the Wild calls back to those games
that came before it with a slew of other place

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:28.440
names as well.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:33.190
Sure, they've shifted around a bit since the
8-bit days, but the names remain the same.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:38.450
Even still, the definitive Zelda guide, Hyrule
Historia, has told us that this series splits

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:42.250
off into multiple timelines… making everything
even more confusing.

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:46.360
Tingel Island is a riff on of the flamboyant
finesser Tingle, who appears throughout the

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:47.360
series.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.191
Toronbo Beach appeared in a slightly different
form in Link's Awakening, and Koholit Rock

00:01:52.191 --> 00:01:56.030
is a clear reference to Koholint Island from
the same game.

00:01:56.030 --> 00:02:00.360
Mido Swamp grabs its title from an obnoxious
character in Ocarina of Time.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.620
And even more Breath of the Wild locations
are straight out of The Adventure of Link:

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:10.140
Rauru Hillside, Ruto Lake, and Saria among
them, all of whom were also revealed as characters

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.830
in Ocarina of Time.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:16.710
And Linebeck Island takes its name from from
the cowardly captain in The Phantom Hourglass.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:17.930
The secret language

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:21.690
An element of Breath of the Wild that has
left players scratching their heads is the

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:26.590
mysterious Sheikah language used on various
items, signs, and descriptions throughout

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:27.590
the game.

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:31.280
While Nintendo could have just given us a
lot of convincing gibberish, like some kind

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.740
of Sigur Ros song, these things actually spell
out words

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:40.020
It's not hard to find a translation key online,
and players have utilized it to decipher hidden

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.020
messages.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:45.530
Most interestingly, one winding bit of text
that appears on the Master Edition boxed version

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:49.350
of Breath of the Wild reads, "The Hyrule Fantasy"
in reverse.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:53.500
True fans know that's the Japanese subtitle
for the original Legend of Zelda.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:57.450
The cool references don't even stay within
the world of Hyrule, however.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:01.740
When installing new abilities to Link's Sheikah
Slate in Breath of the Wild, Sheikah text

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.210
scrolls down the screen.

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:07.780
Some phrases can be translated as "now loading"
and "do not turn off your [slate]," but a

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:11.980
hilarious Easter egg can be found in the sentence
that reads "All your base are..."

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:16.830
This, of course, is a reference to the meme-worthy
English version of Zero Wing, which abysmally

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:21.251
translated the game's original Japanese into
the jumbled-up phrase, "All your base are

00:03:21.251 --> 00:03:22.960
belong to us" when it hit the SEGA Mega Drive
in Europe.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:26.600
And, well, just in case you've forgotten,
this eventually happened:

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:31.460
(amazing Internet song playing)

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.240
Thanks, Internet!

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:35.660
Oman Au

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:39.830
The monks of Breath of the Wild have some
unique names, but one sticks out.

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:46.870
Oman Au is an anagram for Aonuma, and it just
so happens that Eiji Aonuma has been working

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:49.540
on Zelda games since Ocarina of Time.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:54.150
He's currently the series producer and manager
of the entire franchise, so it's a small way

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.920
to immortalize a guy who's already a bit of
a legend.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:03:59.540
The Old Man

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:03.540
One thing that isn't new in Breath of the
Wild is the Old Man, who's clearly a reference

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:08.489
to that other old dude seen in the original
Zelda, who gives Link his first sword...and

00:04:08.489 --> 00:04:09.900
some ominous advice.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.919
This cryptic character shows up again in Breath
of the Wild, again with a fire, and again

00:04:17.919 --> 00:04:19.199
in a small cave.

00:04:19.199 --> 00:04:21.609
Of course, it could be a simple coincidence.

00:04:21.609 --> 00:04:23.629
Weird old men love caves, after all.

00:04:23.629 --> 00:04:26.580
But then, Nintendo stacked another juicy Easter
egg in there.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:31.229
The Sheikah letters pouring out of light beacons
you can place hold a hidden message that links

00:04:31.229 --> 00:04:33.749
players all the way back to the original Zelda.

00:04:33.749 --> 00:04:34.790
The secret words?

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:37.139
"It's dangerous to go alone."

00:04:37.139 --> 00:04:38.539
Just look at the flowers

00:04:38.539 --> 00:04:43.330
If we're talking literal parallels to elder
Zelda games, it doesn't get more literal than

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:44.330
this.

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:47.889
When viewing the Master Sword in Breath of
the Wild, shown on its pedestal in the game's

00:04:47.889 --> 00:04:50.699
official E3 trailer, check out the flowers.

00:04:50.699 --> 00:04:54.229
Now, compare it to where you find the Master
Sword in A Link to the Past.

00:04:54.229 --> 00:04:59.740
Sure, it's just three well-arranged flowers,
but it a fairly clear-cut nod to the franchise's

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:00.740
history.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:05.330
Whether or not it means something significant
is for forum-nerd arguments and YouTube commenters

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:06.460
to decide.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:10.879
And speaking of the sword, Link's weapon-grabbing
experience in Breath of the Wild shares a

00:05:10.879 --> 00:05:14.069
lot of similarities to his journey in A Link
to the Past.

00:05:14.069 --> 00:05:18.229
You'd think he'd be able to tell a magical
sword from a piece of junk by now, but no.

00:05:18.229 --> 00:05:20.569
Maybe he knows a guy who can tell the difference.

00:05:20.569 --> 00:05:24.689
In Breath of the Wild, gamers can grab what
appears to be the Master Sword fairly early

00:05:24.689 --> 00:05:28.479
on in the game, where it sits perched on a
tall rock formation.

00:05:28.479 --> 00:05:33.220
In truth, it's a rusty buzzkill broadsword,
that'll break shortly after you use it a couple

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:34.220
of times.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:38.569
In A Link to the Past, Link also believes
he's stumbled upon the actual Master Sword

00:05:38.569 --> 00:05:42.380
in The Lost Woods, which is weirdly filled
with tons of dummy swords.

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:45.689
Link thinks he's found the for-real blade
when he pulls it up from the ground... but

00:05:45.689 --> 00:05:49.080
it all falls to pieces when it's revealed
it's a total dupe.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:52.400
Next time, just looks for the one that literally
shoots lasers, man.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:53.719
Thanks for watching!

00:05:53.719 --> 00:05:56.729
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:56.729 --> 00:05:59.009
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

